@page "/severity"

<PageTitle>Severity levels</PageTitle>
<h1>Severity levels</h1>
<p>Each rule has a severity (error, warning or info), and each severity shows in its own colour. Only an error blocks a submit: with a warning and an info showing, the form still goes through.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Listing.cs", "SeverityLevels.razor", "SeverityLevels.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>Great synth!</code> into <strong>Description</strong> and leave the field.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> a warning in amber, "Exclamation marks read as shouty".</div></li>
            <li>Type six comma-separated tags into <strong>Tags</strong> and leave the field.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> an info in purple-blue, "More than five tags rarely helps discovery".</div></li>
            <li>Look at the top of the form.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> a <strong>Warnings</strong> panel above an <strong>Info</strong> panel, and nothing for <strong>Title</strong>, though it is required and empty.</div></li>
            <li>Fill in <strong>Title</strong> and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted with 1 warning(s) and 1 info(s)" in the status line.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> Warnings and infos never block a submit; only errors do.</div></li>
            <li>Clear <strong>Title</strong> and leave the field.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Title is required" under the field and in an <strong>Errors</strong> panel above the other two, before you press <strong>Submit</strong> again.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Blocked by errors" in the status line, with the warning and the info still in their panels and colours.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: required; an error, so it blocks a submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong>: an exclamation mark draws a warning, which never blocks.</li>
            <li><strong>Tags</strong>: more than five draws an info, which never blocks.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>Each rule carries a severity. <strong>Title</strong>'s rule is an error, the default. <strong>Description</strong>'s rule sets <code>WithSeverity(Severity.Warning)</code> and <strong>Tags</strong>' sets <code>Severity.Info</code>. An error blocks a submit; a warning or an info shows its message in its own colour and lets the submit through.</p>
        <p>The page places two summaries. The errors summary sets <code>Show="SummaryFilter.Errors"</code> and lists only what blocks; the advisories summary sets <code>Show="SummaryFilter.Advisories"</code> and lists the warnings and infos. Each summary groups its messages by severity, one panel per group. So the errors summary draws an Errors panel and the advisories summary draws Warnings above Info: three panels when every severity shows.</p>
        <p>As you edit, the form shows messages only for the fields you have changed; a field you have not changed shows none. <strong>Title</strong> starts empty and required, but until you change it or press <strong>Submit</strong>, its error stays off the page. So the errors summary draws nothing at first, though <strong>Title</strong> fails its rule.</p>
        <p>A summary with nothing to show draws nothing. Its wrapper and live region stay in the page, empty, so the element a screen reader watches exists before the first message lands in it.</p>
        <p>Two summaries are two live regions, and a message listed in both would be read twice. So the two filters are chosen to share nothing, rather than one of them being left unfiltered. The panels inside a summary are not live regions of their own: one region wraps them all, so however many panels a summary draws, a screen reader hears one announcement from it.</p>
        <p>A submit with only warnings and infos still announces the advisories summary, so a screen reader hears the messages that did not block.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_listing">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary Show="SummaryFilter.Errors" ErrorsHeading="Errors" />
            </div>
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary Show="SummaryFilter.Advisories" WarningsHeading="Warnings" InfosHeading="Info" />
            </div>

            <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _listing.Title" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Title" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Title" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Description" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Description" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Tags (comma-separated) <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_listing.Tags" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Tags" /></div>

            <div class="actions"><button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Submit">Submit</button></div>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
